    An entry is kept when Wiktionary's UK list (via WikiPron), if it has the word, gives
    the same pronunciation; words that list lacks are kept on a stress check alone.
    Wiktionary is used as a check and none of its text is included.

    Provenance, as stated by Britfone's author (README and issue #2 of that repository,
    2023-10-23): the transcriptions started from CMUdict; Wiktionary, Wikipedia and the
    Collins, Oxford, Cambridge and Macmillan dictionaries are named as further sources for
    British forms; the dictionaries "were consulted rather than included", and the only
    sources included, "after heavy modification", were computer-readable ones "(like
    CMUDICT and BNC)". polyg2p relies on that statement and on the MIT licence above; it
    has not audited Britfone entry by entry. The file can be emptied down to its header
    line without touching the code: en-gb then runs on its rules and
    data/en_brit_words.tsv alone (see docs/english-accents.md for what
    that costs in accuracy).

data/en_homograph.tsv, tests/en_homograph_cases.tsv
    Reading table (162 English heteronyms, 326 readings) and test sentences written by the polyg2p authors.

data/en_gb_func.tsv, data/en_brit_words.tsv
    Short lists of British function-word and word forms written by the polyg2p authors.

data/it_suffix.tsv, data/it_open.tsv (Italian stress / vowel-openness statistics)
    Aggregate counts computed from WikiPron's Italian data, which is extracted
    from Wiktionary (CC BY-SA). The tables hold only per-ending statistics, not
    pronunciations; see "Data licensing" in README.md before redistributing.

OPTIONAL data packs (not part of the Apache-2.0 distribution)
    en_homograph.g2p - English heteronym model trained with tools/train_homograph.py on the Wikipedia Homograph
                       Data (Apache-2.0; its sentences are English Wikipedia text, CC BY-SA 3.0), Tatoeba
                       sentences (CC BY 2.0 FR) and WordNet 3.1 (WordNet licence). Whether weights trained on
                       CC BY-SA text are a share-alike derivative is unsettled, so the file is optional and
                       not shipped. A silver-only variant (no Wikipedia text) is built with
                       `train_homograph.py --silver-only`.
    it.lex  - from WikiPron / Wiktionary, CC BY-SA 3.0/4.0
    de.lex  - from german-ipa-dict (devio-at) via ipa-dict, from Wiktionary, CC BY-SA
    These must be distributed under their own share-alike terms.

<lang>-ctx.g2p (heteronym context models, optional pack, not in the wheel)
    Character-level encoders trained on sentences from Tatoeba (https://tatoeba.org, CC BY 2.0 FR),
    labelled by Stanza (Apache-2.0) models that were trained on Universal Dependencies treebanks
    (CC BY-SA 4.0 for Portuguese Bosque; CC BY 4.0 for Italian MarkIT).
    The label chain carries CC BY-SA terms, so these files are distributed apart from the wheel,
    like the CC BY-SA lexicons. See docs/heteronyms.md.

uk.lex (optional Ukrainian stress lexicon, data pack, not in the wheel)
    Built by tools/uk_kaikki.py from the kaikki.org extract of en.wiktionary (stress-marked headwords and inflected
    forms) plus tools/uk_stress.py output from WikiPron / Wiktionary. Wiktionary text is dual-licensed CC BY-SA 4.0
    and GFDL (https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Wiktionary:Copyrights, checked 2026-11-28; the kaikki.org index page
    itself states no licence, only a citation request). The data is share-alike and therefore kept outside the
    Apache-licensed package. Data from dict_uk / ukrainian-word-stress (non-commercial licence) is not used.

data/uk_stem.tsv, data/uk_suffix.tsv, data/uk_stress_exceptions.tsv (Ukrainian stress statistics and exception rows)
    Aggregate counts, and 394 word -> stressed-vowel rows, computed from the stress-marked headwords and inflected forms
    in the kaikki.org extract of en.wiktionary (CC BY-SA 4.0 / GFDL; tools/uk_stress.py --kaikki, tools/uk_exceptions.py).
    Individual stress positions are facts about words; the files hold no Wiktionary text. Same treatment as data/it_suffix.tsv.

it.lex also from tools/it_kaikki.py: stress-marked IPA of single-word headwords in the kaikki.org extract of en.wiktionary
    (CC BY-SA 4.0 / GFDL), merged over the WikiPron entries; optional pack, not in the wheel.

tests/data/zh_poly_fixture.g2p, tests/zh_poly_cases.tsv
    A 64-dimensional test fixture trained on the hand-written sentences in tests/zh_poly_cases.tsv
    (written by the polyg2p authors). No corpus data.

Polyphone models (zh-poly.g2p, yue-poly.g2p, ja-poly.g2p; trained with tools/train_polyphone.py)
    yue-poly.g2p (shipped in data/): trained on the HKCanCor corpus (CC BY 4.0, attribution: Luke & Wong, 2015),
    on Cantonese Common Voice sentences (CC0) and on Cantonese storytelling transcripts (CC0), with readings from a
    teacher fine-tuned from hon9kon9ize/bert-base-cantonese (Apache-2.0 per its model card).
    zh-poly.g2p (optional pack, not in the wheel): trained on the g2pM benchmark and model (Apache-2.0, Kakao Brain;
    sentences from Chinese Wikipedia, CC BY-SA), on Common Voice sentences (CC0), Tatoeba sentences (CC BY 2.0 FR),
    with readings from a teacher fine-tuned from bert-base-chinese (Apache-2.0). Treated as a CC BY-SA derivative.
    ja-poly.g2p: none.

am-gem.tsv - Amharic gemination (unwritten long consonants), optional data pack, not in the wheel:
    built from the hand-marked gemination lists and the Armbruster verb tables of the GFF Data Archive
    (Ge'ez Frontier Foundation, CC BY-SA 4.0, https://github.com/geezorg/data), romanisations from the English
    Wiktionary (CC BY-SA 4.0 / GFDL, kaikki.org extract) and the Amharic Gemination Lexicon v3
    (Dataset.ET, CC BY 4.0, https://huggingface.co/datasets/Chapimenge/amharic-gemination-lexicon; its labels
    were produced by an analyzer, no analyzer code or data is included). Share-alike terms apply to the table.
    The engine runs without it (output is then identical to before).

am-verbs.tsv - Amharic verb gemination rules, optional data pack, not in the wheel:
    root list and templates derived from the Armbruster verb tables of the GFF Data Archive (Ge'ez Frontier
    Foundation, CC BY-SA 4.0, https://github.com/geezorg/data). Share-alike terms apply to the pack. Evaluated
    against hand lists, Wiktionary romanisations and an analyzer-labelled lexicon (evaluation only; none of it is in
    the pack). The engine runs without it (output is then identical to before).
